# E6-D1-FOUNDATION campaign scope selector for .github/workflows/d1-merge-train.yml
# on the docs/replatform-program integration branch. Bump this file to re-trigger
# the live D1 lane (it is on the workflow's push path filter).
#   bounded    = E6F-03 networked smoke + E6F-05 topology network-denial (fast)
#   foundation = the full E6F-01..07 gate campaign (bounded + tests/d1/e6f-*.test.mjs)
AOA_D1_CAMPAIGN=foundation
# Re-trigger nonce: CLI-006 the execution-ownership seam. Tasks 2b/3/4/6 all land in
# server/src ONLY — the suppression seam at the adapter call site, the projector on the
# JOB-005 ingest hook, cancel routing across the five writers, and the outer-finally
# catch — and server/src is NOT on this lane's push path filter, so without this bump
# the live 2-replica D1 lane silently would not rebuild the control-plane image or
# exercise any of it. This bit DEP-009 already.
AOA_D1_RETRIGGER=brw-003a-find-ever-committed
# Second bump, and the reason is the trap itself: the FIRST bump proved the lane on
# 8324e434b, but the adversarial-review fixes that followed (H1 cancel-outcome
# routing, H2 guarded marker write, H3 issue-lock narrowing, M6 wake predicate,
# M4/M5 projector-loop hoist) are ALL server/src-only and therefore invisible to
# this lane's push path filter. A green D1 on the earlier SHA says nothing about
# the code that actually ships. Re-prove the live 2-replica topology on the final
# state.
#
# THIRD bump, same reason again: round 2 of the fix review (the fifth cancel
# writer honouring writeLegacyTerminal, and a fence revoke on marker-write
# failure) is once more server/src-only. The rule this keeps proving: bump AFTER
# the last server/src change, not once per ticket.

# DSK-001 Lane B / B6. The device-credential gate lands in packages/db (the fenced
# secret-resolve decision + its transaction) and server/src (the fixture bridge and the
# integration suites). NEITHER is on this lane's push path filter -- it covers
# docker/**, tests/d1/** and the compose/lib scripts -- so without this bump the live
# 2-replica lane would not rebuild the control-plane image and would exercise none of
# it. The same trap bit CLI-006 three times and DEP-009 once.

# DSK-001 Lanes C and D. The previous bump proved Lane B on dc4b57f6f; 10 files and ~987
# insertions across server/src and packages/db have landed since -- neither is on this
# lane's push path filter, so a green D1 on the earlier SHA says nothing about the code
# that now ships. Two changes in particular need the LIVE topology rather than a unit
# suite:
#
#   C1  executionTargetToAdapterConfig now THROWS for an unmapped target kind instead of
#       returning null. The D1 stack routes real work to real targets; if any of them is a
#       kind the exhaustive switch does not name, the run fails rather than degrading -- and
#       that is precisely the behaviour change worth proving against a live stack.
#   D2  desktopDeviceRoutes is dynamically imported INSIDE the distributed-execution flag
#       block, and the D1 control plane boots with that flag ON. A failed import there is a
#       BOOT failure on both replicas, which no unit test can observe.
#
# Bumped AFTER the last server/src change, per the trap that bit CLI-006 three times.

# DSK-002 Lane A. `server/src/services/folder-grant.ts` gains a REQUIRED `presented`
# parameter and a new `folder-grant-binding.ts` module; `packages/worker-daemon` gains the
# capture-root guard. Neither path is on this lane's push path filter (docker/**,
# tests/d1/** and the compose/lib scripts), so a green D1 on the previous SHA says nothing
# about what now ships.
#
# HONESTY NOTE ON THE RISK, because the bump is cheap and a wrong judgement is not: this
# change is INERT in production. `resolveCapturedPath` and `admitCapture` still have zero
# production callers, so no live request path can reach the new binding and a boot failure
# is not reachable through it. The bump is here because the rule is "touches server/src",
# and every previous attempt to reason a change was too small to matter is exactly how
# CLI-006 got bitten three times and DEP-009 once. The lane is cheaper than being wrong.

# DSK-002 Lane D. The previous bump proved Lane A on 1bd85461a (D1 Merge Train green).
# Since then `server/src` gained the device-local activation policy and a corrected
# deferral comment on the broker port. Same trap, same rule: bump AFTER the last
# server/src change, not once per ticket. Lanes B and C are worker-daemon-only and are
# not what this bump is for.

# REL-004 Lane C (clause 3a). The kill switch is now WIRED into the worker poll, and the
# change is entirely `server/src` + `packages/worker-daemon` + `packages/db` -- none of which
# is on this lane's push path filter. Same rule, bumped AFTER the last server/src change.
#
# THIS ONE IS NOT INERT, unlike the DSK-002 Lane A bump above, and that is the reason to be
# careful rather than cheap about it. Two changes reach a live boot and a live request path:
#
#   1  Migration 0261 grants `aoa_app` SELECT on `instance_settings`, and the manifest entries
#      that must match it land in the same commit. `assertExactServingRoleAuthority` compares
#      the LIVE role's `has_table_privilege` against `appTablePrivileges()` at startup, so a
#      mismatch is `distributed_execution_app_authority` and BOTH replicas refuse to boot.
#      No unit test can observe that; only a live stack can. (It already bit locally: a stale
#      `packages/db/dist/migrations` made the manifest expect a grant the database did not
#      have, and the real server subprocess crashed exactly this way.)
#
#   2  Every poll now performs one extra read before the lease transaction opens, and can
#      answer `drain` instead of `offer`/`no_work`. The two-replica lane is the only place
#      that exercises the read under real concurrency against the real serving role.

# SECOND bump, and the reason is the trap this file keeps documenting. The first bump proved the
# lane on 4d5bcbead and it was GREEN — but `verify` then failed, and the fix moved the grant out
# of `JOB_CONTROL_LEGACY_GRANTS` into its own `KILL_SWITCH_POLICY_APP_GRANTS`, changing BOTH
# `job-control-legacy-grants.ts` and `distributed-execution-databases.ts`. Those are exactly the
# constants `assertExactServingRoleAuthority` compares against the live role at startup, and both
# are `server/src`-only — invisible to this lane's push path filter. A green D1 on the earlier SHA
# says nothing about the manifest that now ships. Bump AFTER the last server/src change.

# REL-004 Lane D. Three `server/src` runtime changes the D1 lane must exercise, none of them on
# its push path filter: (1) `scheduleWarmSandboxReaper` moved OUT of the
# `config.heartbeatSchedulerEnabled` block to module scope, so it now registers on every boot —
# a registration change is exactly the class only a live bring-up can falsify; (2) the sweep gained
# two new arms and two new `environment_leases` queries; (3) `execution-kill-switch-policy.ts`
# gained an always-loaded consumer, so its module graph is no longer dormant. Bumped AFTER the last
# server/src change, per the rule this file keeps re-proving.

# SECOND Lane D bump, and the trap caught me again in the same session. The previous bump proved
# the reaper's registration move and its new arms — but TWO more `server/src` runtime changes
# landed afterwards and would otherwise never reach the live lane:
#
#   1. `environment-runtime.ts` now stamps `metadata.keyGeneration` on the ACQUIRE path. That is a
#      live sandbox-creation path and the riskiest change in the lane; a bookkeeping lookup that
#      failed an acquire would be a real outage, which is precisely what a live bring-up tests.
#   2. `claimTerminalUncleaned`'s predicate changed from `IS DISTINCT FROM 'success'` to
#      `{NULL,'pending'}` — the compare-and-swap fix. A CAS is exactly the thing a two-replica
#      topology falsifies and a single-process test cannot.
#
# The rule this file keeps re-proving: bump AFTER the last server/src change, not once per ticket.

# MIG-005/006/007 Lane A + the Lane B read-only foundation. Two live-path reasons to
# spend the lane rather than one bookkeeping one:
#
#   1. `job-submission.ts` now calls the SHARED `submitJobSourceIdentity` /
#      `submitJobSourceWorkloadType` instead of its own private copies. Behaviour-
#      preserving by inspection, but every real submission on the D1 journey runs
#      through both, and "behaviour-preserving by inspection" is what a live lane is for.
#   2. `with-tenant-tx.ts` / `tenant-context.ts` gained a read-only sibling next to the
#      wrapper that every distributed submission and lease already uses. The addition has
#      no runtime caller yet, but it sits in the file that does.
#
# The comparator + heartbeat seam changes are confined to the default-off shadow branch
# and would not, alone, justify the 45 minutes.

# Lanes C+D. Three LIVE user-visible paths in `server/src` gained a call that did not
# exist before: `cli-mode.ts` (every Commander turn), `aoa-agents/runner.ts` (every crew
# dispatch) and `one-shot-sandbox-cli.ts` (every extraction, compaction and readiness
# probe). Each is guarded, deadline-bounded and inert with the port unregistered — which
# is exactly the claim a live two-replica bring-up is for. `index.ts` also now registers
# the port inside the distributed-execution composition, so the boot path changed.
#
# Bump AFTER the last server/src change, not once per ticket.

# Gate clause 3. A one-line deletion in `server/src/services/issues.ts` on a LIVE cancel path:
# the fifth cancel writer no longer early-returns when no distributed cancellation port is
# registered, so it reaches the convergence block written for exactly that state. Behaviour
# changes only for runs marked `execution_owner = "distributed"`, which is the two-replica
# lane's own territory — the one place a real handed-off run exists to be converged.

# MIG-002 first slice. The rollout source now re-reads per resolution instead of capturing at
# construction, and gained a per-sink `sources` axis. That is the routing decision on the live
# two-replica path: every run resolution and every placement decision goes through it, and the
# heartbeat seam now names its own sink. A regression here routes work to the wrong executor,
# which is precisely what this lane exists to catch.

# The org-capacity release on the legacy-after-convert path. This is a state change on the
# canary routing path the two-replica lane actually drives: a converted attempt that placement
# declines now hands its concurrency slot back instead of holding it forever. Getting it wrong
# in the other direction (releasing a LIVE attempt's slot) would let an org over-subscribe its
# cap, which is exactly the kind of thing a live bring-up catches and a unit test cannot.

# MIG-002 convergence, part 1. `reapExpiredLeases` (packages/db) now additionally returns the
# identities of the attempts it drove to a TERMINAL job outcome. The D1 lane drives this exact
# function: tests/d1/lib/e6f-harness.mjs:1703 posts to the dormant /worker-control/_test/reap
# route so a back-dated lease crosses its deadline synchronously. So this is not an unwired
# change — it is on the one path the two-replica lane actually exercises, and the RETRY
# exclusion (a retried job runs again, so it must NOT be listed) is the kind of thing worth
# proving against real rows and real locks rather than only a fixture.

# MIG-002 convergence, part 2. index.ts now STARTS the JOB-006 lease reaper on a self-scheduling
# loop and projects a run terminal for each attempt it terminalizes, through the same handler the
# worker ingest uses. This is a new background loop that terminalizes work, on the two-replica
# topology, sharing one org enumerator and one projection with the existing paths. If the
# retry-exclusion or the projection's ownership predicate is wrong, this is where two executors
# would show up — a unit test cannot produce that.

# DAT-008 slices 1-4. Two live-path reasons, and they are on the D1 lane's own journey
# rather than adjacent to it:
#
#   1. EVERY lease now performs one extra tenant read (`listActiveExecutionSecretHandles`)
#      inside the offering transaction and threads its result into the envelope. The D1
#      lane's whole reason to exist is real workers taking real leases against the real
#      serving role, and this read runs on that exact path under two-replica concurrency.
#      A envelope that fails `jobEnvelopeV1Schema.safeParse` does not lease at all, so a
#      regression here presents as "the fleet stops taking work" — visible live, invisible
#      to a unit suite.
#   2. Migrations 0262 and 0263 add two columns to `job_secret_handles`, an
#      already-distributed table. They are nullable additive widens and inherit the
#      existing grant, so `assertExactServingRoleAuthority` should be unaffected — but
#      "should be" is the exact phrasing that made the REL-004 Lane C bump necessary, when
#      a stale `packages/db/dist/migrations` made the manifest expect a grant the database
#      did not have and BOTH replicas refused to boot. A live bring-up is how that gets
#      falsified rather than assumed.
#
# The placement mint and the resolve route are NOT the reason for this bump. The mint is
# gated on an active, lease-eligible, selected placement with an agent executor on a cloud
# deployment, and the route has no caller until slice 5 wires the worker. Bumped AFTER the
# last server/src change in this push, per the rule this file keeps re-proving.

# DAT-008 second bump, and the reason is the trap this file exists to document — I
# walked into it hours after writing the paragraph above. The first bump proved
# 4840c04e1 on both lanes. Then the refusal-signal fix landed, and it is `server/src`
# only: `job-placement-transaction.ts` and `execution-secret-handle-mint.ts`. Neither is
# on this lane's push path filter, so the D1 green stands against the PREVIOUS SHA and
# says nothing about what now ships.
#
# It is a small change — a pure predicate and one guarded log line — and "too small to
# matter" is the exact reasoning this file records as having bitten CLI-006 three times
# and DEP-009 once. The concrete risk is not the log text: it is that
# `job-placement-transaction.ts` now imports `../middleware/logger.js`, a NEW module edge
# in the placement graph, and a bad edge there is a boot failure on both replicas that no
# unit suite can observe.
#
# --- BRW-001 (Lane B) ---
# Re-trigger nonce: BRW-001 adds per-workload-type input validation at submit, and the
# wiring lands in `server/src/services/job-submission.ts` — a LIVE path that every job
# submission crosses, and not on this lane's push path filter.
#
# The behaviour change for the paths D1 actually drives is nil BY CONSTRUCTION: the
# validator registry declares a slot for all three frozen workload types but ENFORCES only
# `browser_session`, and a `not_enforced` slot returns the caller's input byte-identically.
# `batch` — which is what D1 submits — is not_enforced.
#
# "Nil by construction" is exactly the kind of claim this file exists to stop me asserting
# instead of proving. The real risk is not the predicate: it is that job-submission.ts now
# carries a NEW static import edge (`./workload-input-validators.js` →
# `./browser-job-config.js` → `@armyofagents/worker-protocol`) on the submission graph.
# That module also runs a module-load ceiling assertion that THROWS. A bad edge or a
# throwing module initialiser there is a boot failure no unit suite can observe.
#
# Lane coordination (§5.7): a bump is last-writer-wins. Lane A's campaign already ran green
# on 39fa9fe34 before this bump, so nothing of theirs is left unproven by it.

# WRK-008 slice 1. A NEW ROUTE REGISTRATION inside the distributed-execution block in
# server/src/routes/execution-targets.ts, plus a new service read. server/src is not on
# this lane's push path filter.
#
# The route has no caller until slice 2, so by the "no runtime caller" half of the rule
# this would not qualify. It is bumped anyway, and the reason is specific rather than
# cautious: the registration is guarded by `opts.workerSession`, and the D1 control
# plane boots WITH distributed execution on, so the route is registered on both
# replicas at boot. A malformed registration or a bad import edge there is a BOOT
# FAILURE on both replicas, which no unit suite can observe and which is exactly what a
# live two-replica bring-up exists to falsify.
#
# Lane coordination, and a correction to how this file has been described. Lane B touched
# campaign.env for BRW-001 without changing the assignment VALUE, and I initially read that
# as leaving their change unproven. It does not. `docker/images/build.sh` runs on EVERY
# invocation of this lane and rebuilds control-plane + worker from the checked-out source,
# so the value is not a build-cache buster: its jobs are to TRIGGER the workflow (this file
# is on the lane's push path filter) and to select campaign scope. Touching the file is
# sufficient; changing the value is convention, and a useful one because it makes the
# reason for each trigger greppable.

# SVC-001 (Lane B, E9). The desired-state service schema lands in packages/db (migration 0264 +
# the service_generations table) and server/src (the ACL/RLS/policy registration the startup gate
# reads, the enforced service workload validator, and server-stamped service identity). NEITHER
# tree is on this lane's push path filter -- it covers docker/**, tests/d1/** and the compose/lib
# scripts -- so without this bump the live 2-replica lane would not rebuild the control-plane
# image and would prove none of it.
#
# Bumped AFTER the last server/src change (the POLICY_COUNTS key-order fix), per the rule this
# file keeps proving. The order fix is exactly the kind of change that MUST be proven live: it
# governs whether a distributed-execution server starts at all, and it failed only on the
# flag-ON path that this lane is the only thing to exercise.

# BRW-003a (Lane B, E8). Splits findCommitted into findCommitted (download readability,
# committed only) and findEverCommitted (the Rule #7 immutability guard, which must also
# count expired). Lands in packages/db (the repository) and server/src (the grant-service
# call site) -- NEITHER on this lane push path filter, so without this bump the live
# 2-replica lane would not rebuild the control-plane image and would prove none of it.
#
# Worth proving live specifically because it changes the UPLOAD-GRANT decision, and the
# D1 lane is the only thing that exercises a real presigned round-trip end to end
# (e6f-05-live-minio against MinIO-over-TLS).

# E6F-14 (Lane A, E5). This bump is different from every other entry in this file: it is not
# only "server/src changed and is off the path filter" -- it is that a NEW LIVE TEST EXISTS
# WHICH THIS LANE IS THE ONLY PLACE THAT CAN RUN IT.
#
# tests/d1/e6f-14-orphan-sweep.test.mjs proves the orphan path end to end against real
# PostgreSQL and real MinIO-over-TLS: mint a grant, PUT the bytes, age the intent past its
# expiry, kill the fence, watch the commit refuse `stale_fence`, and assert the object is
# ACTUALLY DELETED from the store and the intent row transitioned to `swept`.
#
# Until now that path was unit-proven with its wiring only GREP-verified, and both result
# docs (DAT-009 slice 2 §6, DAT-011 §6) said so outright: "no integration test proves a real
# orphan is deleted end to end ... that belongs with the D1 lane". A skipping test is not
# evidence, which is the failure class this whole workstream has been about -- so the test is
# worth nothing until this lane runs it.
#
# The server/src changes it exercises, none of which are on the path filter:
#   DAT-009 slice 2  the grant TTL clamp, and `recordArtifactGrantIntent` on the fence-guarded
#                    seam -- a NEW WRITE on the upload-grant mint path, inside the same tenant
#                    transaction, so a failure there refuses the grant rather than orphaning it.
#   DAT-010          retention is now derived control-plane-side at commit and the manifest's
#                    declaration is ignored -- a change to what every committed artifact stores.
#   DAT-011          a new best-effort trigger on BOTH artifact-commit outcomes, plus a new
#                    module edge into worker-control's composition root. A bad edge there is a
#                    boot failure on both replicas that no unit suite can observe.
#   migration 0265   two partial indexes on job_artifacts, an already-distributed table. The
#                    REL-004 Lane C entry above records what a stale packages/db/dist/migrations
#                    did last time: the manifest expected a grant the database did not have and
#                    BOTH replicas refused to boot. Locally verified idempotent (47/47 migration
#                    gates); a live bring-up is how that stops being an assumption.
AOA_D1_RETRIGGER=e6f-14-orphan-sweep-live

# E6F-14 SECOND bump. The first live run FAILED and it was worth every minute — it found
# three things, none of them a production defect:
#
#   1. My own test's manifest omitted the REQUIRED `createdAt`, so the commit answered
#      `malformed` at the protocol layer and never reached the fence check. The test proved
#      nothing about stale_fence. A test that fails for the wrong reason is as useless as one
#      that passes for the wrong reason.
#   2+3. E6F-05's two assertions both read `rows.length` while their own MESSAGES say
#      "committed". That was true only until DAT-009 slice 2 made the mint record a
#      `status='granted'` intent, so a successful commit now legitimately carries TWO rows on
#      the natural key (the partial-uniques are disjoint by design). Corrected to filter on
#      status, and STRENGTHENED rather than relaxed: each now also asserts the intent row is
#      present. The truncated-upload case is the more interesting one — the intent that
#      remains IS the orphan record, and it is exactly what lets the sweep collect the bytes.
#
# Re-proving on the fixed state.
AOA_D1_RETRIGGER=e6f-14-orphan-sweep-live-2

# E6F-14 THIRD bump. Run 2 got further and found two MORE things, both mine:
#
#   1. Back-dating the lease deadlines does NOT make a fence stale — the commit SUCCEEDED.
#      An overdue expires_at only becomes a refusal once the REAPER converts the lease to a
#      terminal state, which is why e6f-09 pairs the two. Now presents a NON-CURRENT fence
#      token on the commit instead, exactly as e6f-08 does: the guard sees what a superseded
#      worker looks like and answers stale_fence deterministically.
#   2. The assertion covering that step was `assert.ok(expired, ...)` — VACUOUSLY TRUE for
#      any object — so it reported success while proving nothing. The harness returns
#      {ok, updated}; I checked neither. Same class this whole workstream is about, in my
#      own test.
#   3. e6f-05 had a THIRD ordering assumption I missed: `rows[0]` is now the GRANTED intent,
#      not the committed row. Every assertion below it is about the committed artifact, so it
#      reads committedRows[0] explicitly rather than relying on ordering that no longer holds.
AOA_D1_RETRIGGER=e6f-14-orphan-sweep-live-3

# E6F-14 FOURTH bump — and this run found a PRODUCTION DEFECT, not another test bug.
# E6F-05 is green on both tests as of run 3; the correction there is done.
#
# THE DEFECT: a stale fence is refused by resolveWorkerFenceContext, NOT by
# commitArtifactVersion. lockLeaseAckContext looks the lease up BY THE PRESENTED FENCE
# TOKEN, so a superseded fence finds no row and throws JobLeasingError("stale_fence") --
# which never reaches the catch around commitArtifactVersion where DAT-011 put the sweep
# trigger. The sweep therefore NEVER FIRED on a stale-fence refusal: the exact event it was
# designed around, and the exact moment an orphan is created.
#
# Grep verified the trigger had a caller. It could not tell me the caller was UNREACHABLE on
# that path, and no unit test could either -- the control flow only diverges against a real
# lease in a real database. This is what the lane is for.
#
# Fixed in server/src/services/artifact-commit.ts (server/src is off this lane path filter,
# hence the bump), and E6F-14 now asserts the real refusal SHAPE: a top-level denial envelope
# with code=stale_fence, never {outcome:"rejected"}.
AOA_D1_RETRIGGER=e6f-14-orphan-sweep-live-4


# WRK-017 — the first CI-exercised container ENROL on d1, and the reason for this bump is
# different in kind from every other entry above. It is not "server/src changed and is off the
# path filter": everything WRK-017 touches (docker/**, docker-compose.d1.yml, tests/d1/**) IS on
# the filter, so the lane fires without a nonce.
#
# It is here because THE LANE WAS RED and nobody was waiting on the verdict. Measured on
# 2026-09-03: d1-merge-train failed on c3d26657d, 07ed2cc42 and b6e02a478 — five days, three
# merges — all with the same *Build split D1 images* failure, because DEP-011 Slice 1 added a
# workspace DEVdependency to `server` and `pnpm --filter "server..."` traverses dev edges the
# deps stage never installs. `d1-merge-train` is not a required check, so `ci-required` never
# saw it. See E6-F010. The control-plane Dockerfile fix ships with this bump; this line records
# that the run it triggers is the FIRST honest verdict this lane has produced since 2026-08-25.
#
# What the run must show, beyond green: `migrate` logging the enrolment seed, worker-b's
# container-host reaching "worker-daemon enrolled", and tests/d1/container-enrol.test.mjs passing
# in the BOUNDED set (it runs in both scopes deliberately).
AOA_D1_RETRIGGER=wrk-017-first-container-enrol
